<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You can start with a simple question: &#8220;What did I work on yesterday afternoon?&#8221;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For example, ask, &#8220;Which of last week&#8217;s activities are still unassigned?&#8221; Then give it a specific job: &#8220;File the Figma ones under Design.&#8221; Those are requests you can make in the same conversation, rather than working through the activity list by hand.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Before it files those activities, the agent can show a dry run with the number of activities affected and their total time. Review that preview before it makes changes. You still decide what belongs in each project.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You can also have the agent check a proposed rule against existing activity before adding it. That includes checking what your current rules already cover. Once a useful rule is in place, Timing can file matching work automatically.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Timing can keep notes about your projects and conventions for the agent to read in later sessions, too.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Mac app&#8217;s MCP server is currently in beta and requires Timing Connect after the trial. The connection runs on your Mac; the AI provider you connect processes data under its own privacy policy.</p>



